th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/real-analytic-function.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/real-analytic-function.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition on $a$ is required before $f$ can be real analytic at $a$?","options":["$a$ must be the largest point of $E$","$a$ must be outside $E$","$a$ must be an interior point of $E$","$a$ must be an endpoint of $E$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f008818050a82afcb26228b9","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A function on $E=(0,2)$ is represented by its Taylor series about $a=1$ whenever $|x-1|<1$. Which listed interval is a valid open neighbourhood of $1$ for that representation?","options":["$(2,3)$","$(0.5,1.5)$","$[0,2]$","$(1,3)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aa4e54b1bcb87e57f5a40671","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $E = [0,1]$, why is $f\\colon E\\to\\mathbb{R}$ not real analytic on $E$?","options":["$E$ contains infinitely many points","$f$ is real-valued","$E$ is not open","$E$ is bounded"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c0d8d515d812c6a6b77bac1","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_005","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is real analytic at $x=1$?","options":["$f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{-1/(x-1)^2}$ for $x\\neq1$, $f(1)=0$","$f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{\\sqrt x}$ on $[0,\\infty)$","$f(x)=|\\mathrm{e}^x-\\mathrm{e}|$ on $\\mathbb{R}$","$f(x)=|\\ln x|$ on $(0,\\infty)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"70e39abeab40240cfa48351f","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $E = (0,1]$ and $a = 1$. Why can $f\\colon E\\to\\mathbb{R}$ not be real analytic at $a$ under the given definition?","options":["$E$ contains more than one point","Power series cannot be centred at the point $1$","The value $f(1)$ must be zero","$a = 1$ is not an interior point of $E$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fadadbaf0012baabc2022563","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What must represent a real analytic function near a point $a$?","options":["A Taylor polynomial of some finite degree $k$ that equals $f$ near $a$.","A power series centred at $a$ equal to $f$ on some $(a-r,a+r)$.","A power series centred at $a$ converging near $a$, possibly not to $f$.","A polynomial in $(x-a)$ that equals $f$ at the single point $x=a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"849e9a7fe367018f80b89844","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the difference between being smooth and being real analytic?","options":["Smoothness requires equality with a local power series; analyticity need not.","Both only require all derivatives to exist, so the two notions coincide.","Analyticity requires equality with a local power series; smoothness need not.","Analyticity needs only finitely many derivatives; smoothness needs all of them."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4302f973f52449bec10bf01","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_007_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ and $g$ be real analytic on $\\mathbb{R}$. Which of the following functions is NOT guaranteed to be real analytic on $\\mathbb{R}$ by the closure properties?","options":["$|f|$","$f\\circ g$","$f\\cdot g$","$f+g$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"941dba50698489f67027a402","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_012_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ be real analytic at $a$ with $f(x)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_n(x-a)^n$ on $(a-r,a+r)\\subseteq E$, where $0<r<\\infty$. On which of these sets is the series guaranteed to converge uniformly to $f$?","options":["$[a-r/2,\\,a+r/2]$","$[a-r,\\,a+r]$","$(a-r,\\,a+r)$","Every closed interval contained in $E$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1621ae171a34e21eb61d1363","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_016_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The function $f(x)=1/(1+x^2)$ is real analytic on $\\mathbb{R}$. Its power series at $0$ is $\\sum_{n\\ge0}(-1)^nx^{2n}$, with radius of convergence $1$. Which statement is correct?","options":["$f$ is analytic at $x=2$, with a different power series centred at $2$.","$f$ is not analytic at $x=2$, since the series at $0$ diverges there.","The series at $0$ converges to $f$ on all of $\\mathbb{R}$, since $f$ is analytic on $\\mathbb{R}$.","$f$ is real analytic only on $(-1,1)$, where its series at $0$ converges."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4eed47d04c71cd50deed0c5f","time_limit":55}]}}